WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:08.600
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>From WBEZ Chicago, It's This American Life. I'm Ike Sriskandarajah in for Ira Glass.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>来自芝加哥WBEZ，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾克·斯里斯坎达拉贾（Ike Sriskandarajah），替艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）主持节目。

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:13.080
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Pablo, does this feel like an Icarus story to you?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>巴勃罗，这让你觉得像是个伊卡洛斯的故事吗？

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:15.400
<v Pablo Manriquez>Maybe, potentially.
<v Pablo Manriquez>也许，有可能。

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:17.480
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>What part?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>哪一部分？

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:25.880
<v Pablo Manriquez>Well, Icarus dies. Not that part.
<v Pablo Manriquez>嗯，伊卡洛斯死了。不是那一部分。

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:40.600
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>This is Pablo Manriquez, and he recently survived his own personal Greek tragedy. He's a reporter in Washington, DC. I've known him for years. And this all began when a ripped-open package showed up on his front stoop.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这是巴勃罗·曼里克（Pablo Manriquez），他最近亲身经历了一场属于他自己的希腊悲剧。他是华盛顿特区的一名记者，我认识他很多年了。这一切始于他家门廊上出现的一个被撕开的包裹。

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:56.720
<v Pablo Manriquez>It's late winter, 2022. I find a box outside of my apartment. Inside is a canvas, the four primary colors, a paintbrush, and a palette. It just seemed like a sign.
<v Pablo Manriquez>那是2022年的冬末。我在公寓外发现了一个盒子。里面有一块画布、四种原色颜料、一把画笔和一个调色盘。这看起来就像是一个征兆。

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.790
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Yeah. And that's like-- what's the sign?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>是啊。那就像是——什么征兆呢？

00:00:59.790 --> 00:01:16.150
<v Pablo Manriquez>It was just, I thought to myself, somebody painted every painting in the Capitol and the White House and the Department of Justice. All these agency buildings, they have oil paintings. And yet, in the 15 years that I've been in Washington, I've never met an oil painter.
<v Pablo Manriquez>我当时心想，有人为国会大厦、白宫和司法部画了所有的画。所有这些机构大楼里都有油画。然而，在我住在华盛顿的15年里，我从未见过一位油画家。

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:23.010
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>He had this light bulb moment. That could be him.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>他灵光一现。那个人可以是自己。

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:55.330
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>It wasn't the first time he decided to become a new Pablo. When we first met, he was doing PR work. But he decided he wanted to be a reporter no matter what. So at 37, he moved into a flophouse and began freelancing for small publications. And now he spends his days chasing down politicians in the halls of Congress. He made it happen. So with these art supplies, he began to imagine this other life for himself. And he made a Pablo kind of plan.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这并不是他第一次决定成为一个新的巴勃罗。我们刚认识时，他在做公关工作。但他下定决心无论如何都要成为一名记者。所以37岁那年，他搬进了一家廉价旅馆，开始为小刊物做自由撰稿人。现在，他每天都在国会大厅里追逐政客。他成功了。所以有了这些艺术用品，他开始为自己构想另一种生活。他制定了一个“巴勃罗式”的计划。

00:01:55.330 --> 00:02:14.660
<v Pablo Manriquez>So I was like, if I become an oil painter, I would basically be the only oil painter I know and, in some sense, have a monopoly on oil painting in Washington. You know what I mean? If you want an oil painting, especially a portrait, you have to come to me.
<v Pablo Manriquez>所以我当时想，如果我成为一名油画家，我基本上就是我所认识的唯一一名油画家，在某种意义上，垄断了华盛顿的油画业务。你明白我的意思吗？如果你想要一幅油画，尤其是肖像画，你就得来找我。

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:29.460
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>What Pablo lacks in skill and experience he compensates for with heat-seeking opportunism and blinding self-confidence. It can rub people the wrong way, but I appreciate the hustle.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>巴勃罗在技巧和经验上的欠缺，用他那种追逐机会的劲头和盲目的自信弥补了。这可能会让人不舒服，但我很欣赏这种拼劲。

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:45.180
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Pablo, it's so funny to me that you're trying to pick one career in-- I can say it --a dying industry by supplementing your income with another dying industry.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>巴勃罗，我觉得很有趣的是，你试图在一个——我可以直说——夕阳行业里选择一个职业，同时还用另一个夕阳行业来增加收入。

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:52.860
<v Pablo Manriquez>Reporting, an artist, it's like the two poorest. It didn't make any sense to a lot of people.
<v Pablo Manriquez>报道，艺术家，这就像是两个最贫穷的行业。这对很多人来说都毫无意义。

00:02:52.860 --> 00:03:47.830
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>But it made sense to Pablo. Pablo looked down at the new tools in his hands and then tilted his head skyward. Even as Icarus did before him, he saw a way to soar where no mortal had dared to soar. Our show today, myths in real life. What happened next to Pablo, and other daring actions by mortals challenging their small lives. And a cameo by a real life god because the gods get mad if you don't include them, and I'm too busy to get smote. Not today, Hades. Stay with us.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>但这对巴勃罗来说是有意义的。巴勃罗低头看着手中新的工具，然后抬头望向天空。正如伊卡洛斯当年所做的那样，他看到了一条凡人从未敢尝试的翱翔之路。我们今天的节目，现实生活中的神话。接下来巴勃罗发生了什么，以及凡人们挑战平凡生活的其他大胆举动。还有一个真实神灵的客串，因为如果你不把他们包括在内，神灵会生气的，而且我太忙了，没空被雷劈。今天不行，哈迪斯。请继续收听。

00:03:47.830 --> 00:04:20.280
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>It's This American Life, Act 1, Icarus. So Pablo Manriquez, in Washington, DC, had received these art supplies, perhaps discarded by an Amazon package thief, or perhaps a gift from the gods. And he figured, there's all these big egos in town who would love to be memorialized in oil portraits. Well, I could do that for them. Even though Pablo himself had never painted a portrait in his life, or painted anything for that matter.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这里是《美国生活》，第一幕，伊卡洛斯。巴勃罗·曼里克在华盛顿特区收到了这些艺术用品，也许是被亚马逊包裹窃贼丢弃的，又或者是神灵赐予的礼物。他想，城里有这么多大人物，他们肯定很乐意被画成油画肖像。好吧，我可以为他们做这件事。尽管巴勃罗本人从未画过肖像，或者说，他从未画过任何东西。

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:26.480
<v Pablo Manriquez>I had a clear vision in mind of what I needed to do. I needed to learn how to paint a face.
<v Pablo Manriquez>我心中有一个清晰的愿景，知道自己需要做什么。我需要学会如何画人脸。

00:04:26.480 --> 00:05:01.440
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Politicians have them. We can't sell a portrait without one. Pablo finds a YouTube channel called Paint Coach. It has a 10-minute crash course. He follows along. He starts moving paint around on the canvas, and he's hooked. In his head, he's already competing with the legends who are hanging on walls around town. He just needs a first subject to paint. It's got to be someone widely recognizable, someone distinct and iconic. So he picks Mitch McConnell.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>政客们都有脸。没有脸，我们就卖不出肖像画。巴勃罗找到了一个叫“绘画教练”（Paint Coach）的YouTube频道。它有一个10分钟的速成课程。他跟着练习。他开始在画布上涂抹颜料，然后就上瘾了。在他脑海里，他已经在和城里墙上挂着的那些传奇人物竞争了。他只需要第一个绘画对象。必须是一个广为人知、独特且具有标志性的人物。所以他选择了米奇·麦康奈尔（Mitch McConnell）。

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:37.800
<v Pablo Manriquez>He's always had super, super loyal staffers. I mean, I imagine he's-- according to his book, The Long Game, he's run out all his enemies and installed all of his friends. So I was hoping to find Mitch McConnell fans to buy Mitch McConnell paintings. Another thing I did, I looked up on Etsy, I looked up on the typical places where you would go to buy an oil painting, and I couldn't find any oil paintings of Mitch McConnell. If I painted five paintings of Mitch McConnell in a week, I had the only five paintings of Mitch McConnell in existence for sale. Right?
<v Pablo Manriquez>他一直有超级、超级忠诚的幕僚。我的意思是，据他的书《长远博弈》（The Long Game）所述，他赶走了所有的敌人，安插了自己的朋友。所以我希望能找到米奇·麦康奈尔的粉丝来购买米奇·麦康奈尔的画作。我还做了另一件事，我在Etsy上搜索，查看了通常购买油画的地方，但我找不到任何米奇·麦康奈尔的油画。如果我一周画五幅米奇·麦康奈尔的画，那我就是市面上仅有这五幅米奇·麦康奈尔画作的人。对吧？

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:47.960
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>After a few tries, he gets one that looks enough like Mitch McConnell. So he posts a few pictures of his progress, and someone notices. The next day--
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>尝试了几次之后，他画出了一幅足够像米奇·麦康奈尔的作品。于是他在网上发布了一些绘画进度照片，有人注意到了。第二天——

00:05:47.960 --> 00:06:09.810
<v Pablo Manriquez>I was walking through the Senate, and one of his aides kind of came out of the woodwork and was just like, hey, man, these paintings of our boss that you're posting on Instagram are really cool, but could you maybe paint him smiling? I was just like, boom. Hell yeah, I can paint him smiling. I can paint him smiling all day long.
<v Pablo Manriquez>我正走在参议院里，他的一位助手不知从哪冒出来，说道：“嘿，哥们，你在Instagram上发的那些我们老板的画真的很酷，但你能把他画得微笑一点吗？”我当时就觉得，太棒了。当然可以，我可以把他画得微笑。我可以画他笑一整天。

00:06:09.810 --> 00:06:34.610
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Have you ever had a picture in your office of your boss smiling at you? If you have, you might be living in a country where the name "leader" is preceded by "great" or "supreme." I'd like to take this moment to say, I am grateful to work here at This American Life, where the company reimburses half the cost of the smiling portrait of Ira that we are forced to hang in our offices.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>你的办公室里有没有一张老板对你微笑的照片？如果有，你可能生活在一个“领袖”前面带有“伟大”或“最高”头衔的国家。我想借此机会说，我很感激能在《美国生活》工作，公司报销了我们被迫挂在办公室里的艾拉微笑肖像画的一半费用。

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:50.140
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Pablo delivers his first smiling Senator to Mitch McConnell's staffer. And then he gets a request for another, and another. He's 18 Mitch McConnells deep before he stops to catch his breath.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>巴勃罗把他的第一个微笑参议员交给了麦康奈尔的助手。然后他得到了第二份请求，接着是第三份。在停下来喘口气之前，他已经画了18个米奇·麦康奈尔了。

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:57.660
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Were those Mitch McConnells-- in your opinion, were they good?
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>在你看，那些米奇·麦康奈尔的画好吗？

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:03.700
<v Pablo Manriquez>I can't think of a single Mitch McConnell I ever did that was good.
<v Pablo Manriquez>我画的每一个米奇·麦康奈尔，我都想不出哪一个称得上好。

00:07:03.700 --> 00:07:31.700
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>But they were good enough. He sold the first small one for $25, the next one for $50. Eventually, he settled on $500 for 24-by-36-inch paintings. Pablo is becoming a prolific painter. He paints Nancy Pelosi, Ilhan Omar, other politicians. He takes a commission for a lobbyist's dog. Just by his own force of personality, he's making it happen.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>但它们足够好了。他以25美元卖出了第一幅小的，接着以50美元卖出了下一幅。最终，他把24乘36英寸的画定在了500美元。巴勃罗正在成为一名多产的画家。他画南希·佩洛西（Nancy Pelosi）、伊尔汗·奥马尔（Ilhan Omar）和其他政客。他还接受了一位说客给狗画肖像的委托。仅仅凭他个人的性格魅力，他做到了。

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:49.210
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>At the State of the Union, Pablo set up an easel in Statuary Hall. That's overlooking where all the members of Congress and the Supreme Court justices pass through. And Pablo, in his white painter's coat with headphones on, he captured the scene on canvas live.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>在国情咨文演讲期间，巴勃罗在雕像大厅搭起了一个画架。那里正对着所有国会议员和最高法院大法官经过的地方。巴勃罗穿着他那件白色画家外套，戴着耳机，现场捕捉着画面。

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.470
<v Reporter>This is something that is a side project of yours?
<v Reporter>这是你的副业吗？

00:07:52.470 --> 00:08:04.070
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>C-span interviewed him as he painted. And then late last year, in December, he got a phone call from a friend's number, someone who used to be a policy advisor in the executive branch.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>当他在作画时，C-SPAN采访了他。去年年底，12月，他接到了一个朋友号码打来的电话，对方曾是行政部门的一名政策顾问。

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:28.030
<v Pablo Manriquez>I picked up. And I was just like, hey, man, what do you need? I'm kind of busy. I hear on the other end of the line, hey, Pablo, you're a great artist. This is Joe Biden. I'm like, bull-[BLEEP], man. Very funny, right? He's like, no, no, no, I'm serious. Hey, Jill, put it on FaceTime. And bloop, bloop bloop. The FaceTime noise plays. And I'm looking at Dr. Jill Biden.
<v Pablo Manriquez>我接了电话，我说：“嘿，伙计，有什么事吗？我很忙。”我听到电话那头说：“嘿，巴勃罗，你是一位伟大的艺术家。我是乔·拜登（Joe Biden）。”我回答：“胡扯，伙计。真好笑，对吧？”他说：“不，不，不，我是认真的。嘿，吉尔（Jill），开启FaceTime。”然后，“嘟，嘟，嘟”，FaceTime的声音响起。我看到了吉尔·拜登博士。

00:08:28.030 --> 00:08:42.750
<v Pablo Manriquez>And I'm like, oh my God. I'm so sorry, ma'am. I'm so sorry for cursing. I did not mean to say BS in front of you. Yeah, this is real. And she hands the phone back over to Joe Biden. And he was just like, you're a really good artist. This is a really great portrait of Jill and me.
<v Pablo Manriquez>我当时说：“天哪。真的很抱歉，夫人。我很抱歉刚才骂了脏话。我没想在您面前说胡扯。是的，这是真的。”她把手机递回给乔·拜登。他只是说：“你真的是个很棒的艺术家。这是一幅我和吉尔的非常棒的肖像画。”

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:49.260
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>If you've been wondering what Joe Biden's been up to since leaving office, well, mystery solved.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>如果你一直想知道乔·拜登离任后在忙些什么，好吧，谜底解开了。

00:08:49.260 --> 00:09:24.280
<v Pablo Manriquez>He knew the photo that it came from. It was from the 1970s, when they first met. So it was an oil painting of them when they were young. And I was on FaceTime with the president. There were probably 25 reporters in the press gallery filing their stories. And pretty soon, they were all gathered around me. And we were all talking to Joe Biden-- The Washington Post, the AP, the Bloomberg, HuffPost, were all talking-- and Fox News, Daily Caller, we're all looking into the camera and talking to Joe Biden. And he's just telling everybody how bomb my painting is. I was like, that was awesome. That was awesome.
<v Pablo Manriquez>他认出了照片的来源。那是20世纪70年代，他们刚认识的时候。所以这是一幅他们年轻时的油画。我当时正在和总统FaceTime。新闻发布室里可能有25名记者在写稿。很快，他们都围在了我身边。我们都在和乔·拜登交谈——《华盛顿邮报》、美联社、彭博社、赫芬顿邮报都在说话——福克斯新闻、每日传讯（Daily Caller）都在看镜头和乔·拜登交谈。他一直在告诉所有人我的画有多棒。我当时想，太棒了，这真是太棒了。

00:09:24.280 --> 00:10:04.530
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>This is the part of the story when Icarus, with wings cobbled together with wax and feathers, experiences the miracle of flight. Hey, I can see my Minotaur from here. Well, for many mortals, this level of success is thrilling and enough. But not for Pablo. Pablo has a bigger goal. He wants one of his paintings officially hung in the Capitol, approved by a committee and hung by an entity known as the architect of the Capitol, the only people allowed to drive a nail in the wall. Pablo wants to be immortalized in these halls of power.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这就是故事中，伊卡洛斯用蜡和羽毛拼凑出的翅膀，体验飞行奇迹的那一部分。嘿，从这儿能看到我的弥诺陶洛斯。好吧，对许多凡人来说，这种程度的成功是令人兴奋且满足的。但对巴勃罗来说不然。巴勃罗有更大的目标。他希望自己的画能被正式挂在国会大厦里，经过委员会批准，由所谓的“国会大厦建筑师”（Architect of the Capitol）挂上，因为只有这些人被允许在墙上钉钉子。巴勃罗想要在这些权力的殿堂中流芳百世。

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:16.290
<v Pablo Manriquez>I have no legacy. Like, you know what I mean? And while no one might remember any story I write ever, right, the painting will be there for centuries. Centuries, if it's on the wall.
<v Pablo Manriquez>我没有什么遗产。你知道我的意思吗？虽然可能没人会记得我写的任何故事，但画作会在那里保存几个世纪。几个世纪，只要它挂在墙上。

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:47.100
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Maybe you've been wondering this whole time, what do these paintings look like? So to be totally honest, Pablo has improved a lot. But he's doing work that looks like it's made by a talented art student, maybe a folk artist. The colors are pleasing. You can always tell who it is he's painting. But it's not, you know, John Trumbull's painting of the founders signing the Declaration of Independence, or any of the other museum grade paintings hanging in the Capitol rotunda.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>也许你一直很好奇，这些画到底长什么样？老实说，巴勃罗进步了很多。但他画出来的作品看起来就像是一个有才华的艺术系学生，或者民间艺人画的。颜色令人愉悦。你总是能认出他画的是谁。但它并不是约翰·特朗布尔（John Trumbull）画的那些签署《独立宣言》的开国元勋，也不是国会大厦圆顶大厅里挂着的那些博物馆级的画作。

00:10:47.100 --> 00:11:31.580
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Of course, that does not stop Pablo, and a couple of months ago, he saw a way to make his dream happen. In February, the House voted to rename the press gallery after Frederick Douglass, who, among many other things, was also the first Black reporter to cover the Capitol. There was going to be a renaming ceremony and press conference. Well, it just so happened that Pablo had made a portrait of Frederick Douglass. It had been leaning against the wall in the press gallery for the last two years. So Pablo quickly hatched a plan. He's going to grab his painting, sneak his painting into the room where the ceremony is going to take place, and slip it onto a table where everyone can see it.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>当然，这并没有阻止巴勃罗。几个月前，他找到了实现梦想的方法。二月份，众议院投票决定将新闻发布室以弗雷德里克·道格拉斯（Frederick Douglass）的名字命名，他不仅是废奴主义者，还是报道国会大厦的第一位黑人记者。当时要举行更名仪式和新闻发布会。恰好巴勃罗画了一幅弗雷德里克·道格拉斯的肖像。这幅画已经在新闻发布室的墙边靠了两年了。于是巴勃罗迅速制定了一个计划。他要带上他的画，悄悄溜进举行仪式的房间，把它塞到一张桌子上，让大家都能看到。

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:50.030
<v Pablo Manriquez>I scoped out the room. I was like, oh, there are artifacts about Frederick Douglass here. Like, there was this ledger of sales and stuff like that under glass. I was like, I'm going to go grab my painting and put it on the table. And I did. It's like a pop art portrait of Frederick Douglass with a yellow background, like a distinct yellow background.
<v Pablo Manriquez>我查看了房间。我心想，哦，这里有关于弗雷德里克·道格拉斯的文物。比如，玻璃柜下有销售账目之类的东西。我心想，我要去把我的画拿过来，放在桌子上。我真的这么做了。那是一幅带有黄色背景的波普艺术风格的弗雷德里克·道格拉斯肖像。

00:11:50.030 --> 00:12:24.030
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>It's a pretty big canvas, nearly life-sized. And it sticks out compared to, say, the items on loan from the Library of Congress. But nobody says anything. Then the VIPs come in. There's the Speaker of the House, Mike Johnson, Representative Byron Donalds, the bill's sponsor. And there's even a living descendant of Frederick Douglass. Traditionally, at a press conference like this, the press are there to cover the event, not insert themselves into it. But Pablo is Pablo.
