<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Pablo was not discouraged by this decision. Of course he wasn't. He was like, well, if they don't like this Frederick Douglass, I'm going to make them a much bigger Frederick Douglass. And if they don't like that, I'll paint a smaller one. And I'll keep painting and donating Frederick Douglasses until the dam breaks and they all go up. I thought that sounded like a lot of work, at least more than asking them why they said no.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>巴勃罗并没有因为这个决定而气馁。他当然不会。他想：“好吧，如果他们不喜欢这幅弗雷德里克·道格拉斯，我就给他们画一幅更大的。如果他们还是不喜欢，我就再画一幅更小的。我会一直画下去，不断捐赠弗雷德里克·道格拉斯的画像，直到大坝决堤，让它们全部挂上去。”我觉得这听起来工作量很大，至少比问他们为什么拒绝要辛苦得多。

00:14:02.600 --> 00:14:14.020
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>So I reached out and got hold of the meeting minutes. The problem was not about the size or the quality or anything like that. The problem was that Pablo had crossed a line.
