00:18:39.090 --> 00:19:09.570
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>You know how in Greek mythology there's Hades, where everyone goes when they die? You gotta cross the River Styx to get there. And then there's this gate with this three-headed dog, Cerberus. He's guarding the entrance, and he's supposed to make sure only actually dead people enter. This story is about a real person in America who stood at those very gates, which is not the easiest job, it turns out. At least not right now. Nadia Reiman talked to him about it.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>你知道希腊神话中有一个哈迪斯（冥界），每个人死后都会去那里吗？你得穿过冥河才能到达那里。那里有一个守着大门的三头狗，刻耳柏洛斯。它负责把守入口，确保只有真正的死者才能进入。这个故事是关于一个在美国的真实人物，他站在了那扇大门前，事实证明，那不是一份容易的工作。至少现在不是。纳迪亚·雷曼（Nadia Reiman）和他聊了聊这件事。

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:37.060
<v Nadia Reiman>When Jeremiah Schofield started working at the Social Security Administration, the SSA, the first thing his boss said to him was, if you're looking up your ex's records, your own records, or looking up someone famous, don't. We will know. Then they made him read Title V of the US Code Section 552a, The Privacy Act, the actual legal text of it. That's how much they wanted to drive the point home.
<v Nadia Reiman>当杰里迈亚·斯科菲尔德（Jeremiah Schofield）开始在社会保障局（SSA）工作时，他老板对他说的第一句话就是：“如果你想查询你前任的记录、你自己的记录，或者查询名人的记录，千万别这么做。我们会知道的。”然后他们让他阅读了美国法典第5编第552a条《隐私法》的实际法律文本。他们就是这样极力想把这一重点灌输给他。

00:19:37.060 --> 00:20:06.660
<v Nadia Reiman>The Social Security Administration is the caretaker of these truly massive databases of personal information. They use it to send Social Security checks, disability payments. And in order to do that accurately, given how there's over 300 million of us, they keep master files. Jeremiah says they have all sorts of information from all sorts of moments in your life-- where you were born, your Social Security number, your mom's maiden name, your citizenship status, all of it stored in giant files.
<v Nadia Reiman>社会保障局是这些真正庞大的个人信息数据库的保管者。他们用它来发送社会保障支票和残疾赔偿金。为了准确地做到这一点，考虑到我们有超过3亿人口，他们保留了主文件。杰里迈亚说，他们拥有你生命中各个时刻的各种信息——你的出生地、社会保障号码、你母亲的婚前姓氏、你的公民身份，所有这些都存储在巨大的文件中。

00:20:06.660 --> 00:20:15.260
<v Jeremiah Schofield>Your master earnings files will show every job that you ever worked at. And it would show how much money you made at all of those different jobs.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>你的收入主文件会显示你曾经工作过的每一份工作。它会显示你在所有这些不同工作中赚了多少钱。

00:20:15.260 --> 00:20:16.860
<v Nadia Reiman>Every job people have had?
<v Nadia Reiman>人们做过的每一份工作吗？

00:20:16.860 --> 00:20:23.960
<v Jeremiah Schofield>Yep. And then you have the disability control file, which actually gets into disability-related information.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>是的。然后你还有残疾控制文件，它实际上涉及与残疾相关的信息。

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:26.620
<v Nadia Reiman>That's for people who collect disability.
<v Nadia Reiman>那是给领取残疾补助金的人用的。

00:20:26.620 --> 00:20:51.520
<v Jeremiah Schofield>Things like what your disability-- there's listings of codes for if you have cancer or if you have some other type of ailment. And so it would have all of that information in it. So it's very sensitive information. At Social Security, we've always treated those records as though they're things that they cannot be let out to anybody.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>像你的残疾情况之类的——如果你患有癌症或有其他类型的疾病，文件里会有代码列表。所以它会拥有所有这些信息。这是非常敏感的信息。在社会保障局，我们一直把这些记录视为绝不能泄露给任何人的东西。

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:53.800
<v Nadia Reiman>Like, they contain people's whole lives, basically.
<v Nadia Reiman>就像是，它们基本上包含了人们的整个人生。

00:20:53.800 --> 00:20:58.020
<v Jeremiah Schofield>That's right. Very sensitive moments in their lives.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>没错。他们生活中非常敏感的时刻。

00:20:58.020 --> 00:21:14.420
<v Nadia Reiman>The point of the many rules governing these lists and files is not just privacy, though. It's about making sure all this information is used only for its intended purpose, by the book. And Jeremiah, from what I can tell, is a by-the-book kind of guy.
<v Nadia Reiman>但管理这些列表和文件的众多规则的目的不仅是隐私。而是确保所有这些信息仅按照规定用于其预定目的。据我所知，杰里迈亚是一个按部就班的人。

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:28.150
<v Nadia Reiman>There's one database with arguably more power than the others and a name to match. It's called the Death Master File. It's a master list of all the dead. Well, all the deaths recorded since the dawn of Social Security.
<v Nadia Reiman>有一个数据库的权力和影响力堪称之最，名字也相当贴切。它叫“死亡主文件”（Death Master File）。它是所有死者的主列表。好吧，是自社会保障局成立以来记录的所有死亡人数。

00:21:28.150 --> 00:21:49.350
<v Nadia Reiman>It has millions and millions of names. People get added to it when they die, their name and their date of death. If you get accidentally added to it when you are not dead, it can mess up your Social Security payments, sure, but also your bank accounts, all kinds of things. So Jeremiah and everyone treated this one with extreme care.
<v Nadia Reiman>它有数百万个名字。人们死后会被添加到其中，包括他们的名字和死亡日期。如果你在没死的时候被意外添加进去，这会扰乱你的社会保障金，当然，还有你的银行账户，以及各种各样的事情。所以杰里迈亚和每个人都极度谨慎地处理这个文件。

00:21:49.350 --> 00:22:03.350
<v Nadia Reiman>And one day, Jeremiah says, he was summoned to a meeting. And the first thing he sees when he enters the meeting room, handwritten on all these whiteboards, are the names of all these databases, including the Death Master File.
<v Nadia Reiman>杰里迈亚说，有一天，他被召集去开会。当他进入会议室时，他看到的第一件事就是手写在所有白板上的所有数据库名称，包括死亡主文件。

00:22:03.350 --> 00:22:30.280
<v Nadia Reiman>The whole meeting was kind of strange, actually. Jeremiah was a boss. At this point, he had been there 25 years, his first proper adult job. So he'd been to a lot of meetings, but none like this. It was not put on the official calendar, and the whole thing was very secretive. It was in a half-used office building. There was a guard in the hallway, almost like a bouncer. And in the room, three people with non-government laptops.
<v Nadia Reiman>整个会议相当奇怪，真的。杰里迈亚是一位主管。此时他已经在那里工作了25年，这是他第一份正儿八经的成年工作。所以他参加过很多会议，但从来没有像这次这样的。它没有被列在官方日程表上，整件事非常神秘。它在一栋半闲置的办公大楼里。走廊里有个警卫，就像个保镖。房间里有三个带着非政府笔记本电脑的人。

00:22:30.280 --> 00:22:54.840
<v Nadia Reiman>This was February of last year. And these guys, they worked for DOGE, so-called Department of Government Efficiency, headed by Elon Musk. In the meeting, Jeremiah makes some proposals, some ways Social Security can be more efficient, better IT to update these lists quicker. But he's almost instantly interrupted by one of the DOGE guys, Antonio Gracias, a venture capitalist, now Team Elon.
<v Nadia Reiman>那是去年二月。这些人是为DOGE工作的，也就是所谓的“政府效率部”，由埃隆·马斯克（Elon Musk）领导。在会上，杰里迈亚提出了一些建议，关于社会保障局如何提高效率、利用更好的IT系统更快地更新这些名单的方法。但他几乎立刻被其中一个DOGE成员、风险投资家、现在的埃隆团队成员安东尼奥·格拉西亚斯（Antonio Gracias）打断了。

00:22:54.840 --> 00:23:25.790
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And within two minutes, Mr. Gracias had shut down the conversation and said, we're here to talk about Social Security fraud. And we're looking for a big win. And I said, well, a big win? I mean, I have millions of dollars in potential savings by doing these new IT systems. And he said he was only willing to go to the president with a big win of-- he said $50 billion savings.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>在两分钟内，格拉西亚斯先生终止了谈话，说道：“我们是来讨论社会保障欺诈的。我们正在寻找一个重大的胜利。”我说：“好吧，重大的胜利？我的意思是，通过执行这些新的IT系统，我可以节省数百万美元的资金。”他说他只愿意带着一个“重大胜利”去找总统，他说是节省500亿美元。

