<v Nadia Reiman>What did you say?
<v Nadia Reiman>你是怎么说的？

00:23:27.650 --> 00:23:34.690
<v Jeremiah Schofield>Well, what I said was that I don't think you're going to find $50 billion in fraud.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我当时说的是，我不认为你们能在欺诈中找到500亿美元。

00:23:34.690 --> 00:24:06.590
<v Nadia Reiman>Around this time, Elon Musk announced on X that they might have found some fraud. The fraud was related to the Death Master File. Musk was pointing to a problem with it, namely that there were people who were clearly dead who were not in it, people who seem to be 150 years old, according to other Social Security databases. He posted, quote, "Maybe Twilight is real and there are a lot of vampires collecting Social Security." He even brought it up in the Oval Office with President Trump, before the cameras.
<v Nadia Reiman>大约在这个时候，埃隆·马斯克在X上宣布他们可能发现了一些欺诈行为。欺诈与死亡主文件有关。马斯克指出了一个问题，即名单中有一些人明显已经死了却不在名单里，有些人根据其他社会保障数据库显示似乎已经150岁了。他发帖称：“也许《暮光之城》是真的，有很多吸血鬼在领取社会保障金。”甚至在摄像机前，在椭圆形办公室与特朗普总统讨论过这个问题。

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:14.130
<v Elon Musk>Now, do you know anyone who's 150? I don't. OK? They should be on the Guinness Book of World Records. They're missing out.
<v Elon Musk>现在，你认识任何150岁的人吗？我不认识。好了吗？他们应该被列入吉尼斯世界纪录。他们错过了。

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:16.010
<v Nadia Reiman>Jeremiah saw all this.
<v Nadia Reiman>杰里迈亚看到了这一切。

00:24:16.010 --> 00:24:25.220
<v Jeremiah Schofield>So in layman's terms, I was pissed off that our leaders in the country were saying this. And it was infuriating.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>所以用外行话来说，我们的国家领导人说出这种话让我很生气。这真是令人愤怒。

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:52.940
<v Nadia Reiman>Jeremiah was pissed off because, in layman's terms, [BLEEP]'s complicated. It's true that there were some people who appeared to be 150 not marked as dead, but the Social Security Administration knew that already. And they knew why. Sometimes the death of a person born in 1875 or whatever didn't get reported to the SSA. And without an official death date, that person didn't go on the Death Master File.
<v Nadia Reiman>杰里迈亚很生气，因为用外行话来说，这简直太复杂了。有些人确实看起来有150岁且没有被标记为死亡，这一点是真的，但社会保障局早就知道了。而且他们知道原因。有时，一个1875年或类似年份出生的人死亡时，并没有上报给社会保障局。如果没有正式的死亡日期，那个人就不会出现在死亡主文件里。

00:24:52.940 --> 00:25:08.140
<v Nadia Reiman>Regardless, there was a whole system in place to make sure Social Security payments did not go out to these people. Because when someone is over 100, the Social Security Administration actually contacts them to make sure they're still alive.
<v Nadia Reiman>无论如何，确实有一套完整的系统来确保社会保障金不会发放给这些人。因为当某人超过100岁时，社会保障局实际上会联系他们以确认他们是否仍然活着。

00:25:08.140 --> 00:25:25.750
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And so we have different groupings of technicians within the agency that literally make phone calls to these folks, and in some cases go to people's houses just to make sure-- hey, Jeremiah is over 100 years old. Let's make sure that he's still living and that he's receiving the benefits that he needs to.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>所以我们在机构内有不同分组的技术人员，他们确实会给这些人打电话，在某些情况下还会去人们的家里确认——“嘿，杰里迈亚已经100多岁了。让我们确认他是否还活着，是否正在领取他需要的福利。”

00:25:25.750 --> 00:25:58.590
<v Nadia Reiman>He says some of these 100-plus year olds are happy to have a visitor. Others are like, go away, of course I'm alive. And if there's any fraud, anyone taking in a paycheck for a dead relative, the SSA would catch it. Plus, the SSA had already tried to clean up the Death Master File, specifically chasing down death dates for these 150-plus year olds. But it took a lot of time and resources, and it didn't save the government any money, because it's not like the SSA had been sending checks to these dead people. So the opposite of efficiency.
<v Nadia Reiman>他说，其中一些100多岁的老人很高兴有人来看望他们。其他人则会说：“走开，我当然还活着。”如果有任何欺诈行为，有人为已故亲属代领工资，社会保障局都会发现。此外，社会保障局已经尝试清理死亡主文件，专门追查这些150多岁的老人的死亡日期。但这耗费了大量的时间和资源，并没有为政府省下一分钱，因为社会保障局根本就没有给这些死人发过支票。所以这与效率恰恰相反。

00:25:58.590 --> 00:26:30.560
<v Nadia Reiman>But DOGE still pressed on. They wanted Jeremiah's team to mark these apparent 150-year-olds as dead and mark them now. Just populate the sensitive list with random death dates, which was totally unheard of. And it made Jeremiah a little nervous It seemed like a bad precedent because, remember, if you make a mistake in the Death Master File, say, mark someone dead who isn't, it's not just their Social Security that gets shut off. Anything tied to their Social Security number is now bricked.
<v Nadia Reiman>但DOGE仍然坚持这样做。他们想让杰里迈亚的团队把这些看似150岁的老人标记为死亡，而且要现在就标记。仅仅在敏感名单上随意填上死亡日期，这是完全闻所未闻的。这让杰里迈亚有点紧张。这似乎是个糟糕的先例，因为请记住，如果你在死亡主文件中犯了错误，比如把一个还活着的人标记为死亡，那么不仅仅是他们的社会保障金会被停掉。任何与他们社会保障号码相关的东西现在都彻底报废了。

00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:48.200
<v Nadia Reiman>The person finds out, often in pretty dramatic fashion, that they have been killed off by accident. Like, they go to the grocery store, they try to pay with their credit card, and it doesn't work. Then they try to pay with another credit card. Same thing. So they go home and call the bank. And they're like, what's going on?
<v Nadia Reiman>当事人发现时，往往是以相当戏剧性的方式，发现自己被意外弄死了。就像，他们去杂货店，试图用信用卡支付，结果不行。然后他们尝试用另一张信用卡。还是一样。于是他们回家给银行打电话。他们问：“发生了什么？”

00:26:48.200 --> 00:26:51.790
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And they would have been directed to go to Social Security to fix the problem.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>他们会被指引去社会保障局解决这个问题。

00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:55.720
<v Nadia Reiman>Could they have access to their money in their bank accounts or no?
<v Nadia Reiman>他们能动用银行账户里的钱吗？

00:26:55.720 --> 00:26:58.040
<v Jeremiah Schofield>The bank accounts would have been frozen.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>银行账户会被冻结。

00:26:58.040 --> 00:27:04.660
<v Nadia Reiman>Oh my God. So it's not even that they can't use credit cards. They can't take money that they have in the bank out of the bank at all.
<v Nadia Reiman>天哪。所以不仅仅是他们不能使用信用卡。他们根本无法从银行取出哪怕一点钱。

00:27:04.660 --> 00:27:11.280
<v Jeremiah Schofield>That's right. Everything just stops because everybody thinks that the person is deceased.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>没错。一切都停止了，因为每个人都认为那个人已经去世了。

00:27:11.280 --> 00:27:31.330
<v Nadia Reiman>It's a mess. And now here was DOGE, insisting Jeremiah's team marked these 150-plus year olds as dead. Jeremiah was like, OK, I know we don't have physical proof, but if the date of birth is right, logic says these people are for sure dead. So it didn't seem that risky.
<v Nadia Reiman>这简直是一团糟。现在DOGE坚持要求杰里迈亚的团队把这些150多岁的老人标记为死亡。杰里迈亚当时想，好吧，我知道我们没有实际证据，但如果出生日期是正确的，逻辑上讲这些人肯定已经死了。所以这看起来风险并没有那么大。

00:27:31.330 --> 00:27:51.410
<v Nadia Reiman>They did it methodically, carefully, to avoid making mistakes. But ultimately, they put in fake death dates for all those people. Jeremiah didn't like it, but he did it anyway.
<v Nadia Reiman>他们有条不紊、小心翼翼地做了这件事，以避免出错。但最终，他们为所有这些人填上了伪造的死亡日期。杰里迈亚不喜欢这样，但他还是照做了。

00:27:51.410 --> 00:28:14.530
<v Nadia Reiman>This fixation with the Death Master File, little did Jeremiah know it was just beginning. A few weeks after the 150-year-olds, Jeremiah says his higher-ups contacted him with a request from DHS, the Department of Homeland Security. And this request was unlike anything Jeremiah had seen in his more than 20 years at the agency.
<v Nadia Reiman>这种对死亡主文件的执着，杰里迈亚没料到这仅仅是个开始。在150岁的老人名单事件几周后，杰里迈亚说他的上级联系了他，传达了来自国土安全部（DHS）的请求。这个请求与杰里迈亚在局里工作20多年来见过的任何事情都不同。

00:28:14.530 --> 00:28:31.890
<v Jeremiah Schofield>I got a request from the Deputy Commissioner for Operations, my boss, and said, we have a listing of a little over 6,000 people. You need to come up with a strategy on how we're going to kill off these 6,000 folks, a listing we had received from DHS.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我接到了负责运营的副局长的请求，我的老板说：“我们有一份名单，大概有6000多人。你需要想出一个策略，关于我们如何处理掉这6000个人，这是一份我们从国土安全部收到的名单。”

00:28:31.890 --> 00:28:33.610
<v Nadia Reiman>Kill off? Like--
<v Nadia Reiman>处理掉？就像——

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:41.770
<v Jeremiah Schofield>Yeah, DHS had sent us a list. And the expectation was is that we add a date of death for these individuals.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>是的，国土安全部发给了我们一份名单。预期的结果就是我们为这些个人添加一个死亡日期。

00:28:41.770 --> 00:29:08.050
<v Nadia Reiman>To add these 6,000 or so names to the Death Master File. It was strange because, normally, deaths are reported by funeral homes or family members or some state authority. And also, there was no indication from DHS that these people were dead. They just said, add these people to this list. So Jeremiah and his colleagues were trying to figure out, what's going on, and why these 6,000 in particular?
<v Nadia Reiman>把这6000人左右的名字添加到死亡主文件中。这很奇怪，因为通常情况下，死亡是由殡仪馆、家庭成员或某些州当局上报的。而且，国土安全部没有任何迹象表明这些人已经死亡。他们只是说，把这些人加到这份名单上。所以杰里迈亚和他的同事们试图弄明白，到底发生了什么，为什么偏偏是这6000人？

00:29:08.050 --> 00:29:16.600
<v Nadia Reiman>Like, oh, is a pattern here? Is it all people from one area of the country? There was nothing like that that you were able to see.
<v Nadia Reiman>就像，这有什么规律吗？他们都是来自全国同一个地区的人吗？你看到过类似的情况吗？

00:29:16.600 --> 00:29:26.900
<v Jeremiah Schofield>I did not see it. But there were conversations that happened. And the conversations that happened around the agency indicated that it was mostly Hispanic last names.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我没看到。但确实发生过一些谈话。在局里流传的谈话显示，他们大多是西班牙裔的姓氏。

00:29:26.900 --> 00:29:59.860
<v Pablo Manriquez>DHS also sent a memo from then-Secretary of Homeland Security, Kristi Noem, about, quote, "suspected terrorists" who she said had Social Security numbers and access to our financial system. She asked the SSA to stop it in a way that was, quote, "consistent with law." Jeremiah was pretty sure none of this request to mark 6,000 people as dead was lawful. No proof of death, for one thing. So he says, he goes and asks someone at the Social Security office of General Counsel, the lawyers, who tell him, yes, it is illegal.
<v Pablo Manriquez>国土安全部还发来了一份时任国土安全部部长克里斯蒂·诺姆（Kristi Noem）的备忘录，关于所谓的“涉嫌恐怖分子”，她说这些人拥有社会保障号码并能进入我们的金融系统。她要求社会保障局以一种“符合法律”的方式阻止他们。杰里迈亚非常确定，这个标记6000人死亡的请求完全不合法。首先，就没有死亡证明。所以他说，他去问了社会保障局总法律顾问办公室的律师，律师告诉他，是的，这是非法的。

00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:09.030
<v Jeremiah Schofield>The illegal part of it is is making them all dead. You can't just make anybody dead that's not dead. That's against the law.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>违法的部分在于把他们都标记为死亡。你不能仅仅因为一个人没死就把他弄死。那是违反法律的。

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:25.270
<v Nadia Reiman>So Jeremiah and his team refuse. But then, within a couple of days, they start getting some strange reports from the field offices, workers calling in saying, what's going on? People are showing up here saying we marked them as dead. They're definitely not dead.
<v Nadia Reiman>所以杰里迈亚和他的团队拒绝了。但在几天之内，他们开始收到来自各地方办事处的一些奇怪报告，工作人员打来电话说：“发生了什么事？人们来这里说我们把他们标记为死亡了。他们绝对没有死。”

00:30:25.270 --> 00:30:43.310
<v Jeremiah Schofield>I'm like, did somebody process these 6,000 deaths? And we started to ask around the agency at headquarters, and we found out that there was staff in the Office of Chief Information Officer that had posted the dates of death.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我当时说：“有人处理了这6000人的死亡案例吗？”我们开始在总部向局里打听，我们发现首席信息官办公室的工作人员已经填上了死亡日期。

00:30:43.310 --> 00:31:02.750
<v Nadia Reiman>He knew the Chief Information Officer staff did it because that was the only office that could process these deaths the way they were processed, all at once. Jeremiah says all 6,000-some people had been marked as having died on a single day, March 8, 2025. He couldn't believe they had done this.
<v Nadia Reiman>他知道是首席信息官办公室的人干的，因为只有那个办公室能以这种方式一次性处理这些死亡案件。杰里迈亚说这6000多人的死亡日期都被统一标记为2025年3月8日。他不敢相信他们竟然真的这么做了。

00:31:02.750 --> 00:31:42.120
<v Jeremiah Schofield>I mean, by the agency choosing to do this, we just took a list without any documentation, and we posted these 6,000 deaths. That could have happened to anybody. And within 48 hours, that anybody would have had their lives halted until they could fix it. And it's possible they could fix it in a few days. Or there's been cases where we've seen where it takes months or even a year to fix all of the little things that get screwed up because you got your name on the Death Master File.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我的意思是，由于局里选择这样做，我们接受了一份没有任何文件的名单，并公布了这6000人的死亡。这种事可能发生在任何人身上。在48小时内，那个人的生活就会陷入停滞，直到他们能解决这个问题。他们可能几天内就能解决。或者我们也见过需要几个月甚至一年才能解决所有因名字被列入死亡主文件而搞砸的琐事的案例。

00:31:42.120 --> 00:32:03.970
<v Nadia Reiman>The process of getting resurrected-- that's what they call it when you're accidentally killed in the Death Master File and need to be brought back to life-- it's not simple. You have to go into your Social Security office in person and prove you're alive. And sometimes it takes multiple visits, as you keep encountering new corners of your life where some institution or other is still convinced you're dead.
<v Nadia Reiman>所谓“复活”的过程——当你在死亡主文件中被意外弄死并需要被带回“生命”时，他们是这样称呼的——并不简单。你必须亲自去社会保障局办公室证明你还活着。有时需要多次访问，因为你会不断遇到生活中的新角落，那里的一些机构仍然坚信你已经死了。

00:32:03.970 --> 00:32:30.010
<v Nadia Reiman>Jeremiah doesn't tell the field offices that the 6,000 or so were secretly killed by the Chief Information Officer's office, or that DHS requested it. He's like, let's not dwell on the mess. Let's just fix it. And let's fix it the way we normally would, as any other mistake in the Death Master File. Which meant it was up to each one of these 6,000 or so people to come into their local field office and prove they're alive.
<v Nadia Reiman>杰里迈亚没有告诉地方办事处，这6000人左右是被首席信息官办公室秘密弄死的，或者这是国土安全部的要求。他心想，我们别深究这团乱麻了。我们直接修复它吧。而且我们要像处理死亡主文件中的任何其他错误一样，以正常的方式修复它。这意味着这6000多个人中的每一个人都必须亲自前往当地办事处，证明他们还活着。

00:32:30.010 --> 00:32:40.570
<v Jeremiah Schofield>We saw 30 resurrections happen pretty quickly. And then that number quickly grew within a few weeks to 300 resurrections.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我们很快看到了30次“复活”。然后在几周内，这个数字很快增加到了300次。

00:32:40.570 --> 00:32:44.570
<v Nadia Reiman>So that's what was happening on the ground. Meanwhile, from above--
<v Nadia Reiman>这就是地面上正在发生的事情。同时，从高层——

00:32:44.570 --> 00:32:50.930
<v Jeremiah Schofield>We were getting pressure to say, we told you to kill off the 6,000. We want the 6,000 to remain dead.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我们受到了压力，要求说：“我们告诉过你们处理掉这6000人。我们希望这6000人保持死亡状态。”

00:32:50.930 --> 00:32:54.030
<v Nadia Reiman>DHS was telling you that they wanted the 6,000 to remain dead.
<v Nadia Reiman>国土安全部告诉你他们希望这6000人保持死亡状态？

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:59.370
<v Jeremiah Schofield>That's right. And it was coming down from pretty high up leadership.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>没错。而且这是从非常高层的领导那里传达下来的。

00:32:59.370 --> 00:33:21.140
<v Nadia Reiman>When these 6,000 fake deaths happened, The Washington Post found out and published a big story. So did The New York Times. So as the news breaks, Jeremiah is also dealing with the chaotic media fallout of this. And then something bigger happens. And this part of the story has not been out there before. Jeremiah is the first person to talk about it.
<v Nadia Reiman>当这6000起伪造的死亡事件发生时，《华盛顿邮报》发现了并发表了一篇大报道。《纽约时报》也是。所以当新闻爆发时，杰里迈亚也在处理这件事带来的混乱媒体舆论。然后发生了一件更大的事。故事的这一部分以前从未曝光过。杰里迈亚是第一个谈论这件事的人。

00:33:21.140 --> 00:33:36.500
<v Jeremiah Schofield>So then fast forward a few weeks, and a new request comes in to kill off a new grouping of people. The new request from DHS is for 2.7 million names.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>快进几周，出现了一个新的处理人员的请求。来自国土安全部的新请求是270万人。

00:33:36.500 --> 00:33:57.540
<v Nadia Reiman>DHS wants the SSA to kill off 2.7 million people, like an entire city, mark them all as dead in the Death Master File. Again, like last time, no sign or pretense that they're for real dead. DHS tells Jeremiah's team that these 2.7 million are here illegally, and also--
<v Nadia Reiman>国土安全部希望社会保障局处理掉270万人，就像一个整座城市，在死亡主文件中把他们全部标记为死亡。同样，像上次一样，没有任何迹象或借口证明他们真的死了。国土安全部告诉杰里迈亚的团队，这270万人是非法滞留在这里的，而且——

00:33:57.540 --> 00:34:02.270
<v Jeremiah Schofield>These folks were violent criminals and suspected terrorists.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>这些人是暴力犯罪分子和涉嫌恐怖分子。

00:34:02.270 --> 00:34:11.010
<v Nadia Reiman>But, wait, they told you there were 2.7 million violent criminals and terrorists? That's a lot of violent criminals and suspected terrorists.
<v Nadia Reiman>但是，等等，他们告诉你有270万名暴力犯罪分子和恐怖分子？那是很多暴力犯罪分子和涉嫌恐怖分子啊。

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:25.550
<v Jeremiah Schofield>It is. We were pretty suspect about this 2.7 million list. And so when it came in, we also thought it was a pretty big number. And we wondered whether or not the list was accurate.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>确实是。我们对这份270万人的名单感到非常怀疑。所以当它送来时，我们也觉得这是一个非常大的数字。我们怀疑这份名单是否准确。

00:34:25.550 --> 00:34:54.470
<v Nadia Reiman>Last time this happened, when Jeremiah said, wait a minute, these people are alive, that didn't stop the SSA from marking them as dead. So this time he tries something different. He decides to fact check the claims DHS made about the 2.7 million. They can't look at all 2.7 million people. Privacy concerns, and also, the number is so large. But they do get permission to look at a random sample from the list, 25 people. They check what they can.
<v Nadia Reiman>上次发生这种情况时，当杰里迈亚说：“等一下，这些人还活着”，但这并没有阻止社会保障局将他们标记为死亡。所以这次他尝试了不同的方法。他决定核实国土安全部关于这270万人的说法。他们无法调查所有270万人。隐私问题，而且人数太多。但他们确实获得了查看名单中随机样本的许可，也就是25个人。他们检查了力所能及的内容。

00:34:54.470 --> 00:35:06.280
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And so we went through to check one, are they dead? Two, are they here legally or not legally? And what is their current status?
<v Jeremiah Schofield>所以我们去检查：第一，他们死了吗？第二，他们是合法还是不合法在这里的？他们的现状如何？

00:35:06.280 --> 00:35:13.600
<v Nadia Reiman>When Jeremiah's team ran the numbers, they found that all 25 from the sample were alive. And what's more--
<v Nadia Reiman>当杰里迈亚的团队运行这些数字时，他们发现样本中的所有25个人都还活着。而且更重要的是——

00:35:13.600 --> 00:35:37.560
<v Jeremiah Schofield>When we went through the 25 records, we found 23 of them were records where people were either US citizens or lawful permanent resident or were here legally. And then there were two that more investigation needed to occur because it appeared as though that their alien status had expired.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>当我们查阅这25条记录时，我们发现其中23个人的记录显示这些人要么是美国公民，要么是合法的永久居民，要么是合法在这里的。还有两个案例需要进一步调查，因为看起来他们的外国人身份已经过期了。

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:46.200
<v Nadia Reiman>So, alive. And also, most of them clearly in the country legally. Here's what else he remembers from the sample.
<v Nadia Reiman>所以，他们都活着。而且他们中的大多数显然是合法居住在国家的。这是他从样本中记得的其他事情。

00:35:46.200 --> 00:36:12.290
<v Jeremiah Schofield>But I do remember that it did span a pretty big age community. So it was children all the way up to people in their 70s. There's a couple of cases that I remember that we just thought were super curious. There were some teenagers on the list. And we're like, OK, so teenagers are suspected terrorists and violent criminals? I mean, I guess that could happen, but the likelihood of it happening didn't make sense.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>但我确实记得它涵盖了非常广泛的年龄群体。所以有孩子，一直到70多岁的人。我记得有几个案例，我们觉得非常奇怪。名单上有一些青少年。我们心想，好吧，所以青少年也是涉嫌恐怖分子和暴力犯罪分子？我的意思是，那可能会发生，但发生的可能性让人觉得没道理。

00:36:12.290 --> 00:36:27.090
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And then there was another person on the list that was a person that had been in the United States for over 50 years and was receiving widow benefits. And we're like, OK, so this person's a violent criminal and a suspected terrorist?
<v Jeremiah Schofield>还有名单上的另一个人，是一个在美国待了50多年并领取遗属福利的人。我们心想，好吧，所以这个人也是暴力犯罪分子和涉嫌恐怖分子？

00:36:27.090 --> 00:36:45.650
<v Nadia Reiman>So Jeremiah is like, one, I can't mark people who are alive as dead. Two, they told us these were all people without status, and that seems to not be true. And three, the data I did see from the list makes me side-eye that they're even criminals. Too many red flags in a row. He decides to sound the alarm.
<v Nadia Reiman>所以杰里迈亚想：第一，我不能把活着的人标记为死亡。第二，他们告诉我们这些人都没有合法身份，这似乎不是真的。第三，我从名单上看到的数据让我怀疑他们是否真的是罪犯。红旗信号太多了。他决定敲响警钟。

00:36:45.650 --> 00:37:06.890
<v Jeremiah Schofield>I went to my boss. And my boss at the time was now Stephen Evangelista. He had taken over after my other boss left the agency. And I said, we need to have a meeting with DHS. And we need to talk through a couple of things because we think their list is inaccurate. And that's probably a light way of saying that.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我去找了我的老板。当时我的老板是斯蒂芬·埃文杰利斯塔（Stephen Evangelista）。他在我上一任老板离开局里后接手了。我说：“我们需要和国土安全部开个会。我们需要讨论几件事，因为我们认为他们的名单不准确。”这可能还是比较轻描淡写的说法。

00:37:06.890 --> 00:37:32.690
<v Nadia Reiman>So he calls this meeting. They get DHS on a video call. And on the call, Jeremiah says they present their findings, that the people on the list are alive and seem to be here legally. DHS is like, OK, we'll get you a new list. But Jeremiah has another question. "Logistically," he asks, "if there are mistakes on this or any other list you give us, how would a person on the list by mistake fix it?"
<v Nadia Reiman>于是他安排了这次会议。他们通过视频通话联系了国土安全部。在通话中，杰里迈亚说他们展示了他们的调查结果，即名单上的人还活着，且看起来是合法居住在这里的。国土安全部说：“好吧，我们会给你们一份新名单。”但杰里迈亚还有另一个问题。“从逻辑上讲，”他问，“如果这份名单或你们给我们的任何其他名单上有错误，名单上的人该如何修正？”

00:37:32.690 --> 00:38:05.620
<v Nadia Reiman>And Jon Koval, a DOGE guy at DHS, says, "They would go in person to an SSA office, same as any other mistake. Oh, but when they get there, just flag them over for ICE." Jeremiah says he looked at his coworker, and they were visibly really uncomfortable. ICE is not a place where immigrants who have a mistake on any kind of government papers go to get them straightened out. It's where they go get deported. Jeremiah looks at his boss, Stephen Evangelista. To Jeremiah, DHS's intention is now crystal clear.
<v Nadia Reiman>国土安全部DOGE的一名成员乔恩·科瓦尔（Jon Koval）说：“他们会亲自去社会保障局办事处，和其他错误一样。噢，但当他们到达那里时，直接把他们标记为移交给移民及海关执法局（ICE）。”杰里迈亚说他看了看他的同事，他们明显非常不安。移民及海关执法局可不是移民在政府文件中出现错误时去寻求纠正的地方。那是他们被驱逐出境的地方。杰里迈亚看向他的老板斯蒂芬·埃文杰利斯塔。对杰里迈亚来说，国土安全部的意图现在清晰明了了。

00:38:05.620 --> 00:38:37.340
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And so we were like, OK, thank you for the information. We wrapped up the call. Then we're still in the room. And I look across the table. I remember sitting across the table from Stephen. And I said, "The reason that they're doing this with this 2.7 million is because they're trying to deport all of these people. It's plain and obvious." And Stephen said, "You don't know that. You can't be sure of it. That's too much conjecture. You don't know this definitively."
<v Jeremiah Schofield>于是我们说：“好吧，感谢提供信息。”我们结束了通话。然后我们仍然坐在会议室里。我记得坐在斯蒂芬对面。我说：“他们用这270万人做这件事的原因，是因为他们试图驱逐所有人。这显而易见。”斯蒂芬说：“你不知道那回事。你不能肯定。那太过推测了。你不能确切地知道这一点。”

00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:53.050
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And I'm like, "Come on. We know this. This is why this is happening." He's like, "You just don't know it." So we got into a little back-and-forth in reference to that. And he's like, "OK, how about I just call Jon, and I'll ask him?"
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我当时说：“别闹了。我们知道。这就是这件事发生的原因。”他说：“你就是不知道。”所以我们就此展开了一些争论。他说：“好吧，我直接给乔恩打个电话问问他怎么样？”

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:56.190
<v Nadia Reiman>Jon Koval, the DOGE guy working with DHS.
<v Nadia Reiman>乔恩·科瓦尔，与国土安全部合作的DOGE成员。

00:38:56.190 --> 00:39:25.390
<v Jeremiah Schofield>I'm like, "OK." He's like, "I'm going to call Jon. You guys be quiet." So "you guys" were me and his technical assistant. And, "I'll just have a quick conversation with Jon in reference to this 2.7 million and why." So he pulls out his cell phone, and he calls Jon. He puts Jon on speakerphone. And he says, Jon, can you tell me why we're going through all this effort with these 2.7 million people?
<v Jeremiah Schofield>我说：“好。”他说：“我要给乔恩打电话。你们别出声。”所以“你们”是指我和他的技术助理。“我就关于这270万人以及原因和乔恩进行一次简短的谈话。”于是他拿出手机，拨打了乔恩的电话。他开了免提。他说：“乔恩，你能告诉我我们为什么要在这270万人身上费这么大劲吗？”

00:39:25.390 --> 00:40:08.360
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And Jon said matter of fact terms back. He's like, "Well, there's two outcomes that we're hoping for here. One, we'll make the people's lives so miserable with-- their lives will just be hard to go through because we'll turn off their credit. We'll turn off their credit card. We'll turn off their bank. They'll just want to self-deport. Or two, they'll go into a Social Security office, and we'll have ICE pick them up there. Or you'll refer them over to us, and ICE will pick them up either at USCIS or at ICE, wherever you determine that they should go." Just matter-of-factly.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>乔恩直截了当地回答：“嗯，我们希望有两种结果。第一，我们会让这些人的生活变得极其悲惨——我们会关闭他们的信用，关闭他们的信用卡。我们会关闭他们的银行。他们会想要自己离开。或者第二，他们会去社会保障局办事处，我们会让ICE去那里把他们带走。或者你们把他们转交给我们需要求，ICE会在美国公民及移民服务局或ICE把他们带走，由你们决定把他们送到哪里。”他说得理所当然。

00:40:08.360 --> 00:40:47.730
<v Jeremiah Schofield>And when he said that, I was shocked that he said it so matter-of-factly. I was pretty shocked that that happened. It's one thing to think you know and be like 98% sure, but you don't really know something. It's different when you know it 100% and you just heard it from a chief official over at DHS who also happens to be a DOGE associate. And he made it very plainly clear that this is what he was trying to do and the outcomes that they were seeking.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>当他说出那些话时，我非常震惊，他居然说得那么理所当然。我对他居然真的这么说感到相当震惊。你以为你知道并有98%的把握是一回事，但你并不真的确定。而当你100%确定，且你是从国土安全部的一位高级官员口中听到这些，而他碰巧还是DOGE的成员时，情况就完全不同了。他非常直白地表达了他试图做什么以及他们追求的结果。

00:40:47.730 --> 00:41:30.650
<v Jeremiah Schofield>Stephen was like, thank you for the information, quickly got off the call. My office, I had to go through a different door than Stephen did, out the conference room. He quickly got up. We didn't talk about it. We didn't have an after-the-meeting conversation about it. That was also a little bit shocking because I'm sure my face said, I told you so, in unequivocal terms. And so he got up. He went to his office. I got up, went to mine. And we never talked about it again.
<v Jeremiah Schofield>斯蒂芬说：“谢谢提供信息”，然后迅速结束了通话。我不得不从另一扇门走出会议室，而不是斯蒂芬走的那扇。他迅速站起身。我们没有谈论这件事。会议结束后我们没有进行交流。这也有点令人震惊，因为我相信我的表情已经毫无疑问地表达了“我告诉过你了吧”。他站起身，回到了他的办公室。我站起来，回到了我的办公室。我们再也没有谈论过这件事。

00:41:30.650 --> 00:41:49.980
<v Nadia Reiman>Jeremiah quit the SSA few months later, and now he's filing a whistleblower complaint. The 2.7 million person list was a crisis averted. The SSA didn't add those names to the Death Master File. As far as Jeremiah can tell, they're still alive. But of course, they always were.
<v Nadia Reiman>杰里迈亚几个月后辞职了，现在他正在提交举报人投诉。这270万人的名单是一场被规避的危机。社会保障局没有把那些名字加进死亡主文件。就杰里迈亚所知，他们仍然活着。当然，他们一直都活着。

00:41:49.980 --> 00:42:29.570
<v Nadia Reiman>The government is filled with things like the Death Master File, built for one purpose, but if you're of a certain disposition, usable for something else. A list of the dead, a public service to track who is alive and who is dead, or a tool to kill off anyone you don't like. It's not legal. It flies in the face of rules and laws that have governed the list for decades. It violates the whole name of the list. But that doesn't mean you can't do it anyway.
<v Nadia Reiman>政府充斥着像死亡主文件这样的东西，为了一个目的而建立，但如果你有某种倾向，它就能被用于其他用途。一份死者名单，一个追踪谁活着谁死了的公共服务工具，或者一个用来除掉任何你不喜欢的人的工具。这不合法。它违背了数十年来管理该名单的规则和法律。它违反了名单名称的全部意义。但这并不意味着你不能照样做。

00:42:29.570 --> 00:43:02.710
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Nadia Reiman is a producer on our show. By the way, we did reach out to all the people and agencies Jeremiah names in this story. Only the Department of Homeland Security got back to us. They gave us a statement saying, quote, "The government is finally doing what it should have all along, sharing information across the federal government to solve problems." Two congressional offices are currently looking into the allegations Jeremiah made in his whistleblower claim.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>纳迪亚·雷曼是我们节目的制作人。顺便说一下，我们联系了杰里迈亚在这个故事中提到的所有个人和机构。只有国土安全部给了我们回复。他们发表声明说：“政府终于在做它一直应该做的事情，在联邦政府内部共享信息以解决问题。”两个国会办公室目前正在调查杰里迈亚在举报人投诉中提出的指控。

00:43:02.710 --> 00:43:21.400
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Coming up, one man will suffer through many ordeals to have a private encounter with a God living here on Earth, specifically in Barcelona. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>接下来，一个人将遭受许多磨难，只为与住在这里的一个神灵进行一次私人接触，具体是在巴塞罗那。一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:43:21.400 --> 00:44:06.440
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>It's This American Life. I'm Ike Sriskandarajah. Today's show, Not Today, Hades. Our last act, Act 3, Zeus. He has taken many forms here on Earth. For the last 20 years, millions have been worshipping a man they say performs miracles. They call him many names-- Magician, Messiah, The Chosen One. And he looks, in some ways, kind of anonymous, like nobody special. He's short, 5' 7". He's soft spoken, shy even. The legends about this man are many. But very few of his devotees have ever actually met him, let alone talked with him.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾克·斯里斯坎达拉贾。今天的节目，“今天不行，哈迪斯”。最后一幕，宙斯。他曾以多种形式出现在地球上。在过去的20年里，数百万人一直在崇拜一个人，他们说他创造了奇迹。他们叫他很多名字——魔术师、弥赛亚、天选之子。他在某些方面看起来很普通，就像什么特别的人都不是。他很矮，5英尺7英寸（约1.7米）。他说话声音很轻，甚至有些害羞。关于这个人的传奇有很多。但很少有他的信徒真正见过他，更不用说和他说过话了。

00:44:06.440 --> 00:44:26.500
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Writer Daniel Alarcón is one of them, one of the very few mere mortals to have met Lionel Messi, arguably the greatest soccer player of all time. Daniel talked about it on the podcast he hosts with his longtime friend, who's also a writer, John Green. Daniel adapted it for our show.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>作家丹尼尔·阿拉尔孔（Daniel Alarcón）就是其中之一，是极少数见过莱昂内尔·梅西（Lionel Messi）的凡人之一，梅西可以说是有史以来最伟大的足球运动员。丹尼尔在他与多年好友、同样也是作家的约翰·格林（John Green）共同主持的播客中谈到了这件事。丹尼尔为我们的节目改编了内容。

00:44:26.500 --> 00:44:40.500
<v Daniel Alarcón>So John, once upon a time, there were many, many magazines that were published in the United States and around the world. As you know, I've written for many of them. And magazines, for our younger listeners, are very thin little books.
<v Daniel Alarcón>所以约翰，很久以前，美国和世界各地出版了许多许多杂志。如你所知，我为其中很多杂志写过稿。对于我们年轻的听众来说，杂志是非常薄的小书。

00:44:40.500 --> 00:44:45.340
<v John Green>It's like Instagram, but text-based and printed.
<v John Green>就像Instagram，但基于文字且被打印出来。

00:44:45.340 --> 00:45:04.260
<v Daniel Alarcón>Yes. And there was a specific niche among these that were called glossies. And glossies were the high-end fashion magazines. And the glossies got their name from the glossy paper that they were printed on. So Vanity Fair, GQ, Vogue, those are the glossies. And they were kind of lucrative.
<v Daniel Alarcón>是的。其中有一个特殊的细分领域被称为“光面杂志”（Glossies）。光面杂志是高端时尚杂志。它们的名字来源于打印它们时所用的光面纸。所以《名利场》、《GQ》、《Vogue》就是光面杂志。它们当时相当有利可图。

00:45:04.260 --> 00:45:05.860
<v John Green>Yeah, they paid great.
<v John Green>是的，报酬很高。

00:45:05.860 --> 00:45:29.040
<v Daniel Alarcón>They paid so much money. I had never written for a glossy magazine, I should say, until one of these editors who worked at one of these magazines called me up and asked me if I was interested in writing a profile of Lionel Messi. And of course, I was interested, huge soccer fan, blah blah blah. The problem is that I was about to be married, and the reporting trip would be the week after my wedding.
<v Daniel Alarcón>它们付了很多钱。我应该说，在这些杂志的一位编辑打电话给我，问我是否有兴趣为莱昂内尔·梅西写人物专访之前，我从未为光面杂志写过稿。当然，我有兴趣，我是个巨大的足球迷，等等等等。问题在于我当时快要结婚了，而报道旅行定在我婚礼后的一周。

00:45:29.040 --> 00:45:58.090
<v Daniel Alarcón>So terrible timing. I was told I would get to spend three days with the world's greatest player. I'd get to see a training session. I'd get to go to a game. I'd have full access. And I spoke with Carolina. And she said, you've got to go, you got to do this, and we would do the honeymoon later. And I think you know this, but I think it's worth repeating, just what a genius Lionel Messi is, spoken of in the same breath as Pelé, as Maradona.
<v Daniel Alarcón>时机真是太糟糕了。我被告知我将有三天时间与世界上最伟大的球员在一起。我可以看一场训练，可以去看一场比赛。我有完全的访问权。我和卡罗琳娜（Carolina）谈过。她说：“你必须去，你得去完成这个，我们以后再去度蜜月。”我想你明白这一点，但我认为值得重申，莱昂内尔·梅西是多么天才，他被与贝利（Pelé）和马拉多纳（Maradona）相提并论。

00:45:58.090 --> 00:46:27.750
<v Daniel Alarcón>And I felt like there are people who watch Lionel Messi play a game, and they're like, man, he's good. And there are people like me, who understand it or feel like we understand it on a completely different level. And I thought that there would be a way that I could express to him that my understanding of what he was accomplishing on the pitch was not like, woo hoo, goal. You know? It was a much more sophisticated understanding. And I was like, yeah, no doubt, me and Leo are going to be friends.
<v Daniel Alarcón>我觉得有人看莱昂内尔·梅西比赛时会说：“天哪，他真棒。”而像我这样的人，完全能在另一个层面上理解它或感觉自己理解它。我以为我能向他表达我对他球场上成就的理解不是像那种“呜呼，进球了”。你明白吗？那是更深层次的理解。我当时想，没错，毫无疑问，我和里奥（Leo）会成为朋友的。

00:46:27.750 --> 00:46:29.830
<v John Green>That's totally realistic.
<v John Green>那真是太现实了。

00:46:29.830 --> 00:46:51.120
<v Daniel Alarcón>I was imagining us hanging out at his house, playing Xbox or FIFA or something, and then going to eat some Argentine barbecue somewhere, the grill in Barcelona somewhere, and then riding in his car. And then at the end of the night he'd be like, you know what, man? You're so cool. Cancel your flight back. We're hanging out.
<v Daniel Alarcón>我当时想象着我们在他家聚会，玩Xbox或FIFA之类的，然后去某处吃些阿根廷烤肉，巴塞罗那的某家烤肉店，然后坐他的车。晚上结束时他会说：“你知道吗，伙计？你太酷了。取消你的返程机票吧。我们再聚聚。”

00:46:51.120 --> 00:46:51.700
<v John Green>Yeah.
<v John Green>是的。

00:46:51.700 --> 00:47:09.980
<v Daniel Alarcón>Normal stuff. So this is what I was imagining. And, yeah, so basically, 36 hours after my wedding, I'm on a plane to Barcelona. I arrived at New York. So I flew from San Francisco to New York. I checked my email. And that's when I got the first blow, which was that I would no longer have three days with Lionel Messi. I would have a single day.
<v Daniel Alarcón>很正常的事。所以这就是我当时想象的。是的，所以基本上，我婚礼后36小时，我就坐上了去巴塞罗那的飞机。我到了纽约。所以我从旧金山飞往纽约。我检查了邮件。那是我遭遇的第一次打击，那就是我将不再拥有三天时间与莱昂内尔·梅西在一起。我只有一天。

00:47:09.980 --> 00:47:37.810
<v Daniel Alarcón>Now, I still thought a single day is good. I was like, a day. That's still pretty good. I checked my email again when I got to Barcelona, and now it was no longer a day. It was an hour. So at this point, just to be clear, I had given up my honeymoon to spend an hour with a soccer player. It was just beginning to dawn on me that this was the wrong way to start my marriage, right?
<v Daniel Alarcón>现在，我仍然认为一天也不错。我当时心想，一天。那还是很不错的。我到巴塞罗那后又检查了邮件，现在连一天也没有了。只有一个小时。此时，为了清楚起见，我已经放弃了蜜月去和一个足球运动员待一小时。我开始意识到这是开始我婚姻的错误方式，对吧？

00:47:37.810 --> 00:48:10.630
<v Daniel Alarcón>So here's what happened. I got to Barcelona. I had planned four days in town. Now I've got two extra days to report. So I'm just going to report the [BLEEP] out of this story, which is what I do. So I talked to everybody. I found the guy who took Messi's first photo ID back when Messi was a teenager with long hair and he needed his ID card so he could enter the grounds of the stadium. I found the owners of the Argentine restaurant in the suburb where Messi has his gigantic home. And I got to sit in the booth where Messi sometimes goes to eat.
<v Daniel Alarcón>所以发生了什么。我到了巴塞罗那。我计划在城里待四天。现在我有两天额外的时间来报道。所以我就要全力去报道这个故事，这是我擅长的。所以我采访了所有人。我找到了当年梅西还是长发青少年时为他拍第一张身份证照片的那个人，当时他需要身份证才能进入体育场。我找到了梅西那座巨大豪宅所在的郊区的一家阿根廷餐馆的老板。我还坐在了梅西有时去吃饭的卡座里。

00:48:10.630 --> 00:48:22.910
<v Daniel Alarcón>So anyway, I finally do have my day where I'm going to go meet Lionel Messi. And I'm told to get to the Cuitat Esportiva around 10:00 AM. And of course, I did. Punctually there.
<v Daniel Alarcón>不管怎样，我终于等到了我要去见莱昂内尔·梅西的那一天。我被告知上午10点左右到达体育城（Ciutat Esportiva）。当然，我准时到了。

00:48:22.910 --> 00:48:44.000
<v Daniel Alarcón>And I get there, and I'm like, hey, so I'm a journalist for this fancy glossy magazine. This was all been scheduled. Publicists, editors have been negotiating my arrival. Can I walk around? No, no. No, you can't. I'm like, OK. And they take me to this kind of press room. And they tell me Messi will be there in a few minutes.
<v Daniel Alarcón>我到了那里，说：“嘿，我是这本高级时尚杂志的记者。这一切都已经安排好了。公关人员、编辑都在协商我的到来。我能到处转转吗？”“不，不。不行，你不能。”我心想，好吧。他们把我带到这个像是新闻发布室的地方。他们告诉我梅西几分钟内就会到。

00:48:44.000 --> 00:49:11.380
<v Daniel Alarcón>It's super hot. This is like September. I was not given so much as a glass of water. And I waited, and I waited, and I waited. And then someone came in and told me that I'd be meeting Messi actually, not then, but before afternoon training. And I was like, OK. So can I watch the training? And they're like, no, absolutely not. You cannot watch the training. And I'm just like, what the hell? I'm like, what is this?
<v Daniel Alarcón>那里热得要命。现在是九月。他们连一杯水都没给我。我等啊，等啊，等啊。然后有人进来告诉我，实际上我会见梅西不是那时候，而是在下午训练之前。我心想，好吧。那我能观看训练吗？他们说：“不，绝对不行。你不能看训练。”我当时只想，这到底是什么鬼？心想，这是怎么回事？

00:49:11.380 --> 00:49:30.800
<v Daniel Alarcón>So I kind of like am peeking out the window. And I see Messi and the other players arrive. They have super fancy cars. And naturally, Messi did not come up to speak to me before training. And a little factotum was sent up to tell me that he would come after training. So at this point, training started at 3:00. I've been there, remember, since 10:00.
<v Daniel Alarcón>所以我有点从窗户往外窥探。我看到梅西和其他球员到了。他们开着超级豪华的车。当然，梅西在训练前并没有上来跟我说话。一个小随从被派上来说他训练后会过来。所以此时，训练是3点开始。记住，我从10点就在那里了。

00:49:30.800 --> 00:49:59.330
<v Daniel Alarcón>I've resorted to going to the bathroom and just lapping water into my mouth out of the sink because I have not been given anything. That's nothing. I'm eating wadded up paper towels, just chewing on paper just to trick myself. I'm starving. And then I didn't know what had happened at training that day. I found out later. But I could tell, because I am an astute judge of character, John, as you know, that something bad had happened at training.
<v Daniel Alarcón>我不得不去洗手间，从洗手池里往嘴里舀水喝，因为我什么也没得到。这根本不算什么。我甚至在吃揉成团的纸巾，只是嚼纸来欺骗自己。我快饿死了。然后我当时不知道那天训练发生了什么。我是后来才知道的。但我看得出来，因为正如你所知，约翰，我是个敏锐的性格判断者，那天训练中发生了糟糕的事情。

00:49:59.330 --> 00:50:26.130
<v Daniel Alarcón>I kind of saw the players trudging off with this look of "there but for the grace of God go I." They had seen something. I found out later that the something they had seen was a young player named Ibrahim Afellay had torn his ACL. And so I, of course, was not allowed to watch training. So I didn't see any of this. But I could sense something was wrong.
<v Daniel Alarcón>我看到球员们拖着沉重的步伐走出来，脸上带着“多亏了上帝，没发生在我身上”的表情。他们看到了一些事情。后来我才知道他们看到的事情是一名年轻球员叫易卜拉欣·阿费莱（Ibrahim Afellay）撕裂了前十字韧带。所以当然，我不被允许观看训练。所以我没看到这一切。但我能感觉到出了问题。

00:50:26.130 --> 00:50:41.020
<v Daniel Alarcón>And that is one of those things that I think really affects players' moods because they know that all of them are one bad turn, one injury like that, away from sitting out for a year. You know? And maybe, like in the case of Ibrahim Afellay--
<v Daniel Alarcón>这就是我认为真正影响球员心情的事情之一，因为他们知道他们所有人离坐板凳一年都只有一次坏的转身，一次像那样的伤病距离。你明白吗？也许就像易卜拉欣·阿费莱那样——

00:50:41.020 --> 00:50:42.820
<v John Green>And maybe never quite being the same.
<v John Green>也许永远无法完全恢复到原来的状态。

00:50:42.820 --> 00:50:46.000
<v Daniel Alarcón>Never quite being the same, exactly, which is precisely what happened to Afellay.
<v Daniel Alarcón>完全无法恢复如初，正是这样，这正是发生在阿费莱身上的事情。

00:50:46.000 --> 00:51:05.860
<v Daniel Alarcón>Anyway, at this point, it's past 6:00 PM. So I've been there for a full working day, nothing to eat, super hot. And I'm not one to be dramatic, John, but I was basically being held prisoner at the Barcelona training ground by that point. That's how it felt.
<v Daniel Alarcón>总之，此时已经过了下午6点。我已经在那里待了完整的一个工作日，什么都没吃，热得要命。约翰，我不是那种夸张的人，但到那时为止，我基本上就是被关在巴塞罗那训练基地的囚犯。感觉就是那样。

00:51:05.860 --> 00:51:33.070
<v Daniel Alarcón>So finally, it's just before 7:00 PM when the world's greatest player finally walks in. We sit down at this table. It's this little silver table. There's three chairs. It's me across from Messi. And his publicist is kind of in the middle. And so begins the world's worst interview. [LAUGHTER] It's like, he batted me away like-- I can't remember what I asked him, but he gave me this look like he'd rather be at the dentist.
<v Daniel Alarcón>终于，在下午7点前，世界上最伟大的球员终于进来了。我们坐在这张桌子旁。那是一张银色的小桌子。有三把椅子。我和梅西相对而坐。他的公关人员坐在中间。于是世界上最糟糕的采访开始了。（笑声）他把我拒之门外，就像——我不记得我问了他什么，但他给了我那种他宁愿去看牙医的表情。

00:51:33.070 --> 00:51:56.190
<v Daniel Alarcón>And just looking at me, and I'm disheveled and sweaty. My shirt was a normal size at the beginning of the day, but after eight hours of being held hostage at the Barcelona training ground in a hot September day, the shirt was just stuck to me. And I asked my first question. And he said this thing, and I'm going to imitate an Argentine accent. He said--
<v Daniel Alarcón>他就那样看着我，而我衣衫不整，满头大汗。我的衬衫在白天开始时是正常尺寸的，但在九月炎热的一天被当作人质扣押在巴塞罗那训练基地八小时后，衬衫已经粘在我身上了。我问了第一个问题。他说了一句，我要模仿一下阿根廷口音。他说——

00:51:56.190 --> 00:51:59.950
<v Daniel Alarcón>[SPEAKING SPANISH]
<v Daniel Alarcón>（说西班牙语）

00:51:59.950 --> 00:52:07.070
<v Daniel Alarcón>--which Is basically like, dude, I've been asked this so many times. He's rolling his eyes. And I'm just--
<v Daniel Alarcón>——这基本上就像是，伙计，这个问题我被问过太多次了。他翻了个白眼。而我只是——

00:52:07.070 --> 00:52:08.790
<v John Green>Oh God.
<v John Green>天哪。

00:52:08.790 --> 00:52:35.710
<v Daniel Alarcón>And it got worse from there. It just got worse. I realize that something bad has happened at training. I realize that he's not interested in speaking with me. He doesn't know what a GQ is or what a Vogue is. He doesn't give a [BLEEP]. He doesn't care. He's just like, this weird guy who's all sweaty is sitting here. He's been waiting for me for eight hours, like a freaking stalker. And now he's asking me dumb questions that I've been asked before.
<v Daniel Alarcón>情况从此变得更糟了。变得更糟了。我意识到训练中发生了不好的事情。我意识到他不想跟我说话。他不知道什么是GQ，什么是Vogue。他一点都不在乎。他只是心想，这个浑身是汗的怪人坐在这里。他像个疯狂的跟踪狂一样等了我八个小时。现在他还在问那些我以前就被问过的蠢问题。

00:52:35.710 --> 00:52:58.050
<v Daniel Alarcón>And so he's just being monosyllabic. And at the end of it, it's just devastating. Basically, the publicist just kind of calls time on it, and it's done. Before he walks out, we take this photograph. I ask the publicist if it's OK if we take a photograph. And he said this thing to me. He's like, of course. I know we all have feelings.
<v Daniel Alarcón>所以他只是回答单音节词。到最后，这简直是毁灭性的。基本上，公关人员只是宣布时间到了，这就结束了。在他走出去之前，我们拍了那张照片。我问公关人员能不能拍张照。他对我说了那句话。他说：“当然。我知道我们都有感情。”

00:52:58.050 --> 00:53:05.020
<v John Green>We all have feelings?
<v John Green>我们都有感情？

00:53:05.020 --> 00:53:30.520
<v Daniel Alarcón>The publicist can sense my humiliation, which is actually manifesting as physical pain. Like, at this point, it's just like this is the worst reporting experience of my life, possibly one of the most humiliating experiences of my life at all, of the whole of it. So I took my photo. Messi walks out, immediately, of course, forgets the entire interaction that I have never forgotten.
<v Daniel Alarcón>公关人员能感觉到我的羞辱，这实际上表现为身体上的痛苦。就像，此时此刻这就是我一生中最糟糕的报道经历，可能是我一生中最令我羞辱的经历，整个一生。所以我拍了照。梅西走出去，当然立即就忘记了我从未忘记的整个互动。

00:53:30.520 --> 00:53:44.020
<v Daniel Alarcón>Needless to say, I wrote my draft and then got a very efficient, carefully-worded email from my editor saying that the article was not going to be published and that I could kindly take my kill fee and go away.
<v Daniel Alarcón>不用说，我写了初稿，然后收到了一封极其高效、措辞谨慎的编辑来信，说这篇文章将不会出版，我可以客气地拿走我的“杀费”（即不用稿费）然后离开。

00:53:44.020 --> 00:53:44.840
<v John Green>Brutal.
<v John Green>残酷。

00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:45.720
<v Daniel Alarcón>Yeah.
<v Daniel Alarcón>是的。

00:53:45.720 --> 00:54:01.760
<v John Green>I don't know how to ask this delicately, but even on his best day, Lionel Messi is pretty at arms length when it comes to interviews. And in retrospect, there is something charming about the fact that you thought you were going to get the real Lionel Messi.
<v John Green>我不知道该怎么委婉地问，但即使在最好的日子里，莱昂内尔·梅西在接受采访时也相当保持距离。回想起来，你以为你会得到一个真实的莱昂内尔·梅西，这事确实有某种迷人之处。

00:54:01.760 --> 00:54:36.610
<v Daniel Alarcón>Yeah. I mean, he batted me aside like I was a third-tier defender and went on with his life. And so here I am thinking, man, we're going to vibe on this because we both have this deep emotional and deep intellectual understanding of the game. And basically what he was telling me was like, no, you're down here, and I'm up here.
<v Daniel Alarcón>是的。我的意思是，他把我像三流后卫一样推到一边，然后继续他的生活。所以我在这里心想，伙计，我们会产生共鸣的，因为我们对这场比赛都有深厚的情感和深刻的理解。而他基本上就是在告诉我：不，你在下面，我在上面。

00:54:36.610 --> 00:54:53.170
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Daniel and John's show is called The Away End, which is a soccer podcast about the run-up to the World Cup made by two people who really like books. Emmanuel Dzotsi produced this version for our show.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>丹尼尔和约翰的节目叫《客场》（The Away End），这是一个关于世界杯前夕的足球播客，由两个真正喜欢书的人制作。伊曼纽尔·佐齐（Emmanuel Dzotsi）为我们的节目制作了这一版本。

00:54:53.170 --> 00:55:15.530
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Our program was produced today by me and edited by David Kestenbaum. The people who put together today's show are Phia Bennin, Dana Chivvis, Cassie Howley, Adriene Lilly, Seth Lind, Molly Marcello, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, Robin Reid, Ryan Rumery, Alissa Shipp, Christopher Swetala, Nancy Updike, and Diane Wu.
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>今天的节目由我和大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）制作和编辑。今天整理节目的人员有菲亚·本宁（Phia Bennin）、达娜·奇维斯（Dana Chivvis）、凯西·霍利（Cassie Howley）、阿德里安·莉莉（Adriene Lilly）、塞斯·林德（Seth Lind）、莫莉·马塞洛（Molly Marcello）、凯瑟琳·雷·蒙多（Katherine Rae Mondo）、斯托·尼尔森（Stowe Nelson）、罗宾·里德（Robin Reid）、莱恩·鲁梅里（Ryan Rumery）、阿丽莎·希普（Alissa Shipp）、克里斯托弗·斯威塔拉（Christopher Swetala）、南希·厄普代克（Nancy Updike）和黛安·吴（Diane Wu）。

00:55:15.530 --> 00:55:47.660
<v Ike Sriskandarajah>Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman, and our executive editor is Emanuele Berry. Very special thanks today to Sam McKiniss. Thanks also to all This American Life partners. When you join, you'll get regular, exclusive bonus episodes, listen ad-free, most importantly, help us continue making this show. Join at thisamericanlife.org/lifepartners. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
